From: Richard Spindler <richard@lateralblast.com.au>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 51/55] scp: read the type of the resume reply from its first
 byte

After handling an R reply the source tested *cp for 'S' (skip the file) and
'C' (send the whole file), but cp had been moved past the hash, so what it
looked at was whatever followed the hash: a padding byte from an honest sink,
anything at all from a malicious one. An 'S' there made the source skip the
file, and a 'C' made it send the whole file after it had already seeked into
it, so it read past the end and sent zeros. Look at the first byte of the
reply, which is the type.
---
 scp.c | 9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/scp.c b/scp.c
index ce4cc5e73..c1f4a60d9 100644
--- a/scp.c
+++ b/scp.c
@@ -1707,13 +1707,18 @@ syserr:			run_err("%s: %s", name, strerror(errno));
 					}
 				}
 			}
-			if (*cp == 'S') { /* skip file */
+			/*
+			 * The record's type is its first byte. cp has moved on past
+			 * the hash if this was an R record, and what follows the
+			 * hash is padding, so it is not read as a type.
+			 */
+			if (inbuf[0] == 'S') { /* skip file */
 #ifdef DEBUG
 				fprintf(stderr, "%s: Should be skipping this file\n", hostname);
 #endif
 				goto next;
 			}
-			if (*cp == 'C') { /*transfer entire file*/
+			if (inbuf[0] == 'C') { /*transfer entire file*/
 #ifdef DEBUG
 				fprintf(stderr, "%s: Resending entire file\n", hostname);
 #endif
